// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4): "Every
// potentially-evaluated call to it shall produce a constant expression. If it
// does not, the program is ill-formed." Reading a non-`constexpr` local
// variable is not a constant expression ([expr.const.core]), so the call
// below is ill-formed. Note that §9.1(4) makes *the program* ill-formed --
// the call is not silently demoted to a runtime call, and (unlike
// [expr.const.imm]/3-4 in C++) `main` does not escalate to an immediate
// function.
//
// This is the reason-pinned counterpart of
// 30_constant_evaluation/consteval_call_with_runtime_argument_is_rejected.scpp,
// which asserts only that *some* diagnostic is produced. The accompanying
// .stderr file additionally requires the diagnostic to name the immediate
// function and to state the constant-expression reason, and refuses to accept
// an `internal error`.
//
// Rejection independently confirmed with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which
// reports: "error: call to consteval function 'twice' is not a constant
// expression" / "note: read of non-const variable 'runtime' is not allowed in
// a constant expression".
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS the .stderr pin. scpp does reject
// the program, but with "error: internal error: no generated code for
// resolved function 'twice' called here" -- an internal-compiler-error
// report, not a diagnosis of §9.1(4). The unpinned 30_constant_evaluation
// case named above passes on exactly that internal error today, which is what
// this case exists to expose. Do not relax the pin to admit `internal error`.
consteval int twice(int x) {
    return x * 2;
}

int main() {
    int runtime = 21;
    return twice(runtime);
}
